widget.solution:
def same_truth_table(f, g):
    for p in (True, False):
        for q in (True, False):
            if f(p, q) != g(p, q):
                return False
    return True

explain.text:
Two inputs that are each `True` or `False` give exactly four rows, and the loops visit all of them. So here checking every case is a proof. With whole numbers there are infinitely many cases, and checking some is only evidence.

```python
for p in (True, False):
    for q in (True, False):
        if f(p, q) != g(p, q):
            return False
return True
```
